course: medicine
lesson: 29
title: Vaccinations and why
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: A vaccine is a practice fight for your immune system: it shows the body a killed, weakened or harmless piece of a germ, so the body learns to recognise it and builds antibodies and memory cells.
explain: If the real germ turns up later, the body knows it at once and wins before you get properly ill, which is why a jab protects you for years.
explain: Vaccines also protect people around you: when nearly everyone in a class is immune, a germ like measles can't find enough people to jump to, so babies too young for the jab and children having cancer treatment stay safe; this is herd immunity.
explain: In 1796 Edward Jenner noticed that milkmaids who caught mild cowpox didn't get deadly smallpox, and he made the first vaccine from it; nearly two hundred years later vaccination wiped smallpox off the Earth.
explain: The rows of jabs on the little red book are not random: each one is timed for when a child is most at risk, starting at 8 weeks old.
fact: A vaccine trains the immune system with a killed, weakened or partial version of a germ, or instructions to make a harmless piece of it, so the body fights the real germ quickly later; without a vaccine, the body needs days to learn to fight a new germ, so you can get properly ill first.
fact: A sore arm, a slight fever or tiredness for a day or two after a jab is a normal sign the immune system is responding, and does not mean the vaccine gave you the disease.
fact: In the UK babies have their first vaccines at 8 weeks, against diseases such as whooping cough, because babies are most at risk from them, and their immune systems easily handle several vaccines at once.
fact: Children have booster jabs before starting school, and teenagers get more at secondary school, such as HPV, which protects against some cancers, and meningitis vaccines.
fact: Measles is one of the most infectious diseases known and can cause pneumonia and brain swelling, and it spreads again wherever fewer people are vaccinated; there have been outbreaks in England, Europe and the USA in recent years.
fact: The MMR vaccine protects against measles, mumps and rubella in two doses in early childhood.
fact: The claim that MMR causes autism came from a 1998 study that was found to be dishonest and was withdrawn in 2010; large studies of millions of children found no link.
fact: Herd immunity means that when most people are immune, a germ can't spread easily, protecting those who can't be vaccinated; for measles about 95 in every 100 people need to be immune.
fact: Edward Jenner made the first vaccine in 1796 using cowpox, and the word vaccine comes from the Latin «vacca», cow.
fact: Smallpox killed hundreds of millions of people; after a worldwide vaccination campaign the last natural case was in 1977 and it was declared wiped out in 1980; this was possible because there was a good vaccine and smallpox lived only in people, with no animals to hide in.
fact: Polio can cause paralysis; vaccination has made it rare, and the wild virus is still found mainly in Pakistan and Afghanistan.
fact: Vaccines are tested in the lab and then in trials with thousands of people before approval, and their safety keeps being watched afterwards.
fact: Travellers to some countries need extra vaccines or medicines, such as against typhoid or yellow fever, and a travel nurse advises which ones.
fact: Some families worry about vaccines because of things they read online, and doctors and nurses answer their questions with the evidence.
exercise: How does a vaccine protect you? | It trains the immune system to recognise a germ | It kills every germ in your body for the rest of your life | It makes your blood thicker so germs get stuck | It keeps germs off your skin like a shield
exercise: What does a vaccine usually contain? | A killed, weakened or harmless piece of a germ | A full dose of the real disease, to toughen you up | Antibiotics that kill any germ that comes near | Vitamins and minerals to make you stronger
exercise: Your arm is sore after a jab. What does it mean? | The immune system is responding, normally | The vaccine has given you the disease it was for | The nurse put the needle in the wrong place | You are allergic and must never have jabs again
exercise: When do UK babies have their first jabs? | At 8 weeks | At 5 years, when they start school | At birth only, in the hospital | At 12 years
exercise: Why do babies get vaccines so early? | They are most at risk from diseases like whooping cough | Their arms are softer so the needle goes in more easily | It's easier to hold them still while they're tiny | Babies never get side effects from anything
exercise: Can a baby's immune system handle several vaccines at once? | Yes, easily | No, it overloads and stops working | Only one a year, or it gets confused | Only after age 2, when it is grown
exercise: Which vaccine do teenagers get that protects against some cancers? | HPV | MMR | The flu nasal spray | Tetanus booster
exercise: Why is measles coming back in some places? | Fewer people are vaccinated | The vaccine stopped working on new children | Measles turned into a completely new disease | Hot summers help it spread further
exercise: What does MMR protect against? | Measles, mumps and rubella | Malaria, mumps and rickets | Meningitis, measles and rabies | Mumps, malaria and rubella
exercise: Does MMR cause autism? | No, the study claiming it was dishonest and withdrawn | Yes, a doctor proved it in 1998 and it was never questioned | Yes, in some children who are sensitive to it | Nobody has ever checked whether it does
exercise: What is herd immunity? | When most people are immune, so a germ can't spread easily | When cows and sheep in a field are vaccinated against disease | When a whole class gets ill together | When one strong person protects everyone around them
exercise: Who made the first vaccine, in 1796? | Edward Jenner | Alexander Fleming | Louis Pasteur | Marie Curie
exercise: What did Jenner use to make it? | Cowpox | Mouldy bread | Sea water and salt | Snake venom
exercise: What disease was wiped out by vaccination? | Smallpox | The common cold | Chickenpox | Seasonal flu
exercise: Where is wild polio still found? | Mainly Pakistan and Afghanistan | Everywhere in Europe, every winter | Only in the UK and Ireland | Nowhere at all since about 1900
exercise: How are vaccines checked for safety? | Lab tests, trials with thousands of people and checks afterwards | They are not tested, because they are only small doses | Tested on one brave person, and if they're fine it's sold | Only by the company that makes and sells them
exercise: Why might you need jabs before a trip abroad? | Some diseases exist in other countries | The airline charges you for them | All countries demand ten jabs at the border | To stop you getting jet lag on a long flight
test: Mate: "vaccines are pointless, nobody gets measles anymore". What's the catch? | Measles is rare because of vaccines, and comes back when fewer are vaccinated | He's right, measles is extinct like the dinosaurs and can never come back again | Measles was never dangerous, just a few spots | Only adults catch measles, so children don't need it
test: Lily can't have the measles jab because she's having cancer treatment. How can her class protect her? | By being vaccinated themselves, so measles can't reach her | By all wearing hats and scarves in class | By avoiding her and never sitting next to her at all | By taking vitamin C every morning
test: "my cousin got a fever after his jab so the jab gave him the illness" — right? | No, a mild fever is the immune system responding | Yes, every jab gives you a small dose of the full disease | Yes, but only when jabs are given in winter | No, the fever was caught from the nurse
test: If 1 in 20 children in a town aren't vaccinated against measles, and then many more stop, what happens? | Measles can start spreading again | Nothing changes, the town stays safe | Measles disappears even faster than before | Only the adults in the town get ill
test: Why was smallpox possible to wipe out, while colds are not? | Smallpox had an effective vaccine and only infected humans | Smallpox was a mild disease that nobody minded catching | Colds have been wiped out too, but they keep coming back | Smallpox lived only in cows, so the cows were kept away
test: Online post says MMR causes autism. What's the truth? | The study was dishonest and withdrawn, and huge studies found no link | It's proven true by many doctors around the world for decades now | It's true for boys only, never for girls | Nobody knows anything, it has never been looked at
test: A 6-week-old baby has a cough that won't stop. Why is whooping cough so feared for babies? | Babies are most at risk of serious illness from it | Babies can't cough, so it goes to their tummy | It only affects adults, so it means something else | It's harmless for babies but keeps the family awake
test: Your friend's family doesn't vaccinate. What's best for you to do? | Stay friends and let doctors answer their worries | Shout at them until they change their minds | Stop playing with him in case he's ill | Tell everyone at school that he's dirty
test: In a sci-fi film a vaccine is made overnight and given to everyone untested. Real life? | Vaccines go through lab tests and big trials before approval | Vaccines are always made overnight when there's a new illness | Vaccines are never tested, there isn't time | Only animals get the tested vaccines
test: Why are milkmaids part of the vaccine story? | Jenner saw that cowpox protected them from smallpox | They invented the first needles for injections | They had no germs at all from drinking milk | They drank a special medicine milk that cured smallpox
test: "jabs are just for babies" — fair? | No, children and teenagers get boosters and new vaccines | Yes, only babies, because their blood is new | Yes, after your first birthday there are no more jabs ever | No, only old people over 80 get them
test: If your body met a germ for the first time with no vaccine, why would you get more ill? | The immune system would take days to learn to fight it | Germs are always stronger the first time they meet you | Vaccines make germs weaker everywhere in the world | You'd have no blood cells to fight with
exam: Identify the mechanism by which a vaccine confers protection against disease. | The immune system is trained to recognise the pathogen in advance | The pathogen is destroyed within the bloodstream by antibiotics | The skin is rendered impermeable to pathogens | The body temperature is permanently raised
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the alleged association between the MMR vaccine and autism. | The study alleging it was found to be fraudulent and withdrawn, and no link has been found | The association was conclusively established in 1998 and subsequently confirmed by large investigations | The association is confirmed in respect of boys only | No investigation has ever been undertaken
exam: Ascertain the disease which, pursuant to a worldwide programme of vaccination, was declared eradicated in 1980. | Smallpox | Measles | Poliomyelitis | Seasonal influenza
exam: Whereas a sufficient proportion of a population is immune, determine the consequence for persons who cannot be vaccinated. | They are protected, as the pathogen cannot readily spread | They are placed at greater risk than formerly | They become immune without any exposure to the pathogen | They must be isolated permanently from the population
